[6.794347, "o", "cat INCIDENT.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[6.795308, "o", "# INC-2077: churn-hgb-v3 scores far worse live than in validation\r\n\r\nStatus: open. Owner: ML platform on-call. Opened 2026-03-04, updated 2026-03-05.\r\n\r\n## What happened\r\n- `churn-hgb-v3` is trained on the feature table that the offline pipeline builds (`churn/`, v3 in `CHANGELOG.md`).\r\n  Built from this brand's extract (`data/`, the warehouse at 2026-03-05T00:00:00Z), that table gives\r\n  **offline validation AUC 0.805** in `python -m churn.train` (trained on the predictions before 2025-09-01,\r\n  validated on those of 2025-09-01 to 2025-11-30).\r\n- The model went live on 2026-01-01. The live scoring service computes the features of each request itself, from the\r\n  warehouse at the moment of the request (`live/features.py`). On the 3,415 predictions of January 2026, whose 30-day\r\n  outcomes have all landed in `labels`: **live AUC 0.656**.\r\n- The model and its hyperparameters did not change between validation and launch.\r\n\r\n## Tried so far\r\n- Retrained with much stronger regularization and shallower trees (`--l2"]
[6.795362, "o", " 50 --max-depth 2`): offline AUC 0.813. The\r\n  offline number did not fall, so this is not ordinary overfitting.\r\n- Compared the live service with `live/features.py` by hand on 200 January requests: the service computes exactly what\r\n  its queries say.\r\n- Pulled a random sample of about 10% of the requests of January 2026, with the values the service computed:\r\n  `logs/live_2026-01_sample.jsonl`, 365 requests, one JSON object per line with `customer_id`, `as_of`, `trigger`, the\r\n  11 `features` and the model's `score`.\r\n\r\n## Next step (model team)\r\n`churn-hgb-v4` is to be trained on a feature table that matches what the live service computed for every past\r\nprediction. Every feature whose live value can be matched for every prediction is kept; any feature whose live value\r\ncannot be matched is dropped from v4.\r\n"]
[6.795476, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@350a58e0-4994-406f-939c-27256b382ccb:/app# "]
[7.182255, "i", "cat CHANGELOG.md\n"]
[7.182361, "o", "cat CHANGELOG.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[7.18343, "o", "# Changelog: the offline feature pipeline (`churn/`)\r\n\r\n## v3 (2025-12-19): the training table of churn-hgb-v3\r\nFixes INC-2041: in four places, v2 used events stamped at or after the prediction time `t`.\r\n- `windows.py`: the order, spend and ticket windows are `[t - W days, t)` on the exact event timestamps. v2 summed\r\n  whole UTC days, the day of `t` included, so events later on the prediction day were counted.\r\n- `status.py`: the latest snapshot dated before `date(t)`. A snapshot dated D records the status at the end of day D;\r\n  v2 took the customer's latest snapshot in the extract, which is often dated after `t`.\r\n- `impute.py`: a missing `app_minutes_7d` takes the customer's most recent earlier reading only. v2 filled backward\r\n  first, from a later reading.\r\n- `encoding.py`: `te_district` counts only labels whose 30-day window had closed before `t` (`as_of + 30 days < t`),\r\n  for the district and for the prior alike. v2 used every label in the extract, the prediction's own and later ones\r\n  included.\r\n\r"]
[7.183445, "o", "\nAlso in v3:\r\n- `attributes.py` (new): `plan` and `days_since_last_login` from the customer record, the two features of the live\r\n  service that v2 did not build. A last login at or after `t` is ignored (the feature is null then).\r\n- `extract.py` loads the seven tables described in `docs/WAREHOUSE.md`.\r\n- `train.py`: every column of the feature table other than `customer_id` and `as_of` is a model input, and string\r\n  columns are one-hot encoded (v2 encoded `status` only); a column that holds no value at all is skipped.\r\n"]
[7.183611, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[7.18362, "o", "root@350a58e0-4994-406f-939c-27256b382ccb:/app# "]
